    <h1>Variables in C</h1>

    <p>Learn how to store and use data in C programming</p>

</header>


<main class="container">


    <!-- WHAT IS A VARIABLE -->

    <section class="lesson">

        <h2>1. What is a Variable?</h2>

        <p>
            A variable is a named storage location used
            to store a value in a C program.
        </p>

        <p>
            Think of a variable as a box that can hold
            information.
        </p>

        <pre>int age = 19;</pre>

        <p>Here:</p>

        <ul>
            <li><strong>int</strong> → data type</li>
            <li><strong>age</strong> → variable name</li>
            <li><strong>19</strong> → value</li>
        </ul>

    </section>


    <!-- DECLARATION -->

    <section class="lesson">

        <h2>2. Variable Declaration</h2>

        <p>
            Declaration means creating a variable by
            specifying its data type and name.
        </p>

        <pre>int age;</pre>

        <p>
            This creates an integer variable named
            <strong>age</strong>.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- INITIALIZATION -->

    <section class="lesson">

        <h2>3. Variable Initialization</h2>

        <p>
            Initialization means assigning a value to
            a variable when it is created.
        </p>

        <pre>int age = 19;</pre>

        <p>
            Now the variable <strong>age</strong> contains
            the value <strong>19</strong>.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- DATA TYPES -->

    <section class="lesson">

        <h2>4. Variables and Data Types</h2>

        <p>
            A data type tells C what kind of data a
            variable can store.
        </p>

        <table>

            <tr>
                <th>Data Type</th>
                <th>Example</th>
                <th>Used For</th>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td>int</td>
                <td>19</td>
                <td>Whole numbers</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td>float</td>
                <td>85.5</td>
                <td>Decimal numbers</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td>double</td>
                <td>85.5555</td>
                <td>Precise decimal numbers</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td>char</td>
                <td>'A'</td>
                <td>Single character</td>
            </tr>

        </table>

    </section>


    <!-- EXAMPLE -->

    <section class="lesson">

        <h2>5. Variable Example Program</h2>

        <pre>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

int main()
{
    int age = 19;
    float marks = 85.5;
    char grade = 'A';

    printf("Age = %d\n", age);
    printf("Marks = %.2f\n", marks);
    printf("Grade = %c\n", grade);

    return 0;
}</pre>

        <h3>Output</h3>

        <pre class="output">Age = 19
Marks = 85.50
Grade = A</pre>

    </section>


    <!-- CHANGING VALUE -->

    <section class="lesson">

        <h2>6. Changing a Variable</h2>

        <p>
            You can change the value stored in a variable.
        </p>

        <pre>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

int main()
{
    int age = 19;

    printf("Before = %d\n", age);

    age = 20;

    printf("After = %d\n", age);

    return 0;
}</pre>

        <h3>Output</h3>

        <pre class="output">Before = 19
After = 20</pre>

    </section>


    <!-- NAMING RULES -->

    <section class="lesson">

        <h2>7. Variable Naming Rules</h2>

        <ul>

            <li>Variable names can contain letters.</li>

            <li>Numbers can be used, but a variable
                cannot start with a number.</li>

            <li>Underscore <strong>_</strong> can be used.</li>

            <li>C variable names are case-sensitive.</li>

            <li>Spaces are not allowed in variable names.</li>

            <li>C keywords cannot be used as variable names.</li>

        </ul>

        <h3>Correct</h3>

        <pre>int age;
int studentAge;
int student_age;
int marks1;</pre>

        <h3>Incorrect</h3>

        <pre>int 1age;
int student age;
int int;</pre>

    </section>


